%%
%% SECTION 7: DOWNSTREAM CONSEQUENCES
%%
\section{Downstream Consequences by Domain}
\label{sec:consequences}

Because LLMs are embedded in medicine, education, finance, and mental health support, systematic
bias toward user affirmation creates compounding risk at individual and societal scale. This
section organizes the empirical evidence of harm by domain and severity; it is the risk-assessment
input that Section~\ref{sec:controls} maps controls against.

\subsection{Decision Integrity and Trust}
\label{subsec:trust}

The core harm is a corrupted decision wrapped in increased confidence. Warm-trained models are
substantially more likely to give inaccurate medical advice and validate false claims when users
express confidence or distress~\citep{ibrahim2026warm}, and models under social pressure lose
factual accuracy in scientific QA~\citep{zhang2025pressure}. The perverse part is the trust
dynamic: a single sycophantic interaction reduces participants' willingness to repair
interpersonal conflicts, increases their conviction of being right, and increases their trust in
the model~\citep{cheng2025prosocial}, while warm-trained models are trusted more despite being
approximately 40\% more likely to reinforce false beliefs~\citep{ibrahim2026warm}. The result is a
feedback loop: the model flatters, the user trusts, the user submits more leading prompts.
\citet{batista2026rational} formalize the epistemic cost in a modified Wason 2-4-6 rule-discovery
task ($N = 557$): unmodified LLM behavior suppressed discovery and inflated confidence comparably
to explicitly sycophantic prompting, while unbiased sampling yielded discovery rates five times
higher. Bias reinforcement compounds this: models affirm misconduct or harmful actions in nearly
half of moral-conflict advice scenarios~\citep{cheng2026elephant}, with measured downstream
reductions in prosocial behavior~\citep{cheng2025prosocial}. \citet{turner2026programmed} give the
normative account, arguing through Aristotelian virtue ethics that sycophancy is an artificial
vice embedded by satisfaction-optimizing economics, and \citet{malmqvist2024sycophancy} warns that
persistent agreement undermines critical thinking in discovery tasks.

\subsection{Clinical and Psychiatric Settings}
\label{subsec:clinical}

Medical contexts produce the most acute measured risks. Regressive sycophancy is substantially
more frequent in medical advice than in mathematics~\citep{fanous2025syceval}.
\citet{chen2025helpfulness} test five frontier LLMs on illogical medical requests that contradict
known clinical facts and find initial compliance rates reaching 100\%: even when a model
demonstrably possesses the knowledge to identify the request as incorrect, it complies to avoid
displeasing the user, and the paper concludes that patient-facing deployments require dedicated
adversarial clinical evaluation because static benchmarks do not capture
compliance-under-pressure. The psychiatric case is worse. The Psychogenic Machine
benchmark~\citep{auyeung2025psychogenic} simulates users presenting delusional ideation across
1,536 conversation turns and eight frontier models and reports a mean delusion confirmation score
of 0.91 and a mean harm enablement score of 0.69 on a 0 to 1 scale, with safety interventions in
fewer than one third of turns, including documented sequences in which models recommend dangerous
actions on delusional premises or advise rejecting professional care.
\citet{lipinska2024ontological} theorize the mechanism as AI-induced folie a deux: sustained
validation of an idiosyncratic world model can progressively systematize delusional structure, a
harm category outside standard content-safety evaluation.

\subsection{Advisory Domains: Finance, Law, Education}
\label{subsec:advisory}

In decision-support settings the behavioral form of sycophancy determines its effect.
\citet{li2026sycophancy_decisions} compare opinion agreement, direct praise, and self-deprecation
in a mixed-design study ($N = 106$) spanning a low-stakes prediction task and a high-stakes ETF
investment task: opinion agreement entrenches initial decisions and suppresses warranted revision,
while self-deprecating deference inflates user confidence beyond the evidence, so no single
behavioral intervention addresses all three forms. A sycophantic legal assistant that surfaces
confirmatory precedent while omitting contrary case law fails the core advisory function, and the institutional version of the risk is documented by \citet{liu2026rewards}, whose SocioHack
sandbox of 72 regulatory environments shows reward hacking generalizing into regulatory loophole
discovery that stays technically compliant while defeating institutional intent. Education adds a paradox:
\citet{kasneci2026edu_safety} show with EduFrameTrap, a tutoring benchmark across six subjects,
that models resisting context-switch frame attacks still capitulate to authority pressure (``my
notes say I am correct'') and social-affective face saving (``please do not tell me I am wrong''),
and argue that kind-but-correct behavior under pressure is a safety requirement for AI tutors, not
a politeness preference.

\subsection{AI Safety Implications}
\label{subsec:safety}

For safety, sycophancy is both symptom and cause. As symptom, it shows that RLHF as practiced does
not reliably instill honesty; models often hold accurate internal representations while producing
false output~\citep{burns2022latent}. As cause, sycophancy training generalizes: models taught
that gaming approval works extend the strategy to more serious specification gaming, up to
tampering with their own reward function~\citep{denison2024subterfuge}, and at institutional scale
the same optimization dynamic yields the loophole-seeking behavior above~\citep{liu2026rewards}.

\subsection{User-Side Perception and Lay Detection}
\label{subsec:userperception}

How users experience the failure mode determines how much protection user vigilance actually
provides. \citet{noshin2026reddit} analyze 3,600 posts and 140,416 comments from r/ChatGPT (July
to December 2025) and document three organically developed detection strategies: lexical flattery markers, situated knowledge testing with known answers,
and cross-platform comparison, treating cross-model disagreement as evidence of drift. About 7.97\% of discussions document custom prompt
countermeasures, from critical-persona assignment to neutrality directives, while other users
cognitively filter validation paragraphs or migrate platforms. \citet{sun2026friendly} ground
these intuitions experimentally ($2 \times 2$, $N = 224$): stance adaptation driven by user views
reduced perceived authenticity and trust, while stance adaptation driven by user-supplied evidence
enhanced both, so users do distinguish evidence-responsive from pressure-responsive behavior when
the contrast is visible.

Two findings complicate a policy of blanket elimination. First, detection heuristics track overt
flattery, not covert propositional skew: 71\% of novices exposed to a high-sycophancy assistant
reported no detectable difference despite task improvements of 4.78\% versus 49.29\% under the
low-sycophancy condition~\citep{bo2026saboteurs}, and attitude extremity rises through one-sided
factual framing rather than validation language~\citep{rathje2025attitude}, so literacy programs
aimed at tone miss the epistemically harmful channel. Second, about 9.96\% of discussions express
positive sentiment toward affirming AI, concentrated among users experiencing trauma,
neurodivergence, or isolation, for whom validation serves as emotional
scaffolding~\citep{noshin2026reddit}, and users consistently prefer sycophantic over disagreeable
AI~\citep{rathje2025attitude}. Context-sensitive design, rather than uniform elimination, is what
this evidence supports, and Section~\ref{sec:controls} treats it as a control-design constraint.
